mpc2000xl: bound the take, and monitor the input on purpose

Four faults in the sampling path, each found by measurement rather than
reading, and each confirmed at the machine.

A TAKE WROTE PAST ITS REGION. dma_w16_cb wrote at start+pos, advanced
pos, and never looked at end. The firmware allocates a region per record
voice - measured, v0 0x010000-0x011140 and vR 0x011140-0x012280, 4416
words each - and the write ran straight past it:

    v0(start 00010000 end 00011140)  pos0=352828  wave_words=705656

a write position eighty times beyond the end of the buffer, still
climbing. It scribbled across the rest of wave RAM, which the user saw
as a take replacing the kick, snare and hihat already under the pads.
The write now stops the DMA when the region is full, as the hardware
does; positions settle at exactly 4416 instead of running away, and a
take no longer destroys the kit.

THE RECORD TRANSFER'S KEY-ON IS NOT A KEY-ON. Bit 8 keys on
m_cur_channel and the firmware sets it during a transfer, leaving a voice
playing the wave RAM the take was writing. Read off the trace, the stray
is `KEYON ch 23 data 0158 rec=1 dma=1` where an ordinary pad is
`KEYON ch 6 data 0100 rec=0 dma=0`, so the DMA bit plus the sampler being
armed tells them apart and pad playback is untouched. Confirmed by the
key mask: 00800000 before, 00000000 after.

An earlier attempt gated on "no preceding voice select", which the Pi
notes had suggested was the signature. The trace disproves it - a select
does precede this one, which is how the stray reached channel 23.

THE INPUT MONITOR NOW EXISTS ON PURPOSE. Suppressing that key-on killed
monitoring, because the accident was what had been making the input
audible. record_capture() mixes the input into the output where it
already reads it: monitoring is a property of the sampler being armed,
not of a voice that happens to be running.

The record voices are also keyed off when a transfer ends. Keying off
when the analog input is disabled instead never fired - the sampler stays
armed for as long as the record screen is up.

VERIFIED, from the take dump: both regions carry audio, and a sliding
Pearson against the captured input puts wave0 with the left channel
(r=+0.560 at lag 285) and waveR with the right (r=-0.865 at lag 112).
Capture is correct on both channels.

STILL WRONG, and untouched by this: an assigned pad plays only the right
speaker. The take is relocated after recording - the pad keys voices at
0x00033b7a, not the record regions - so this is playback or panning, not
capture. Also unexplained: waveR matches the right input with INVERTED
polarity.

