+	// A TAKE MUST NOT WRITE PAST THE REGION THE FIRMWARE GAVE IT.
+	//
+	// Nothing bounded this. The firmware programs start and end for both
+	// record voices - measured, v0 0x010000-0x011140 and vR
+	// 0x011140-0x012280, 4416 words each - and the write ran straight on
+	// past them. Caught in the trace with the sampler merely armed:
+	//
+	//     v0(start 00010000 end 00011140)  pos0=617404  wave_words=1234808
+	//
+	// a write position 140 times beyond the end of a 4416-word buffer,
+	// climbing for as long as the record screen was up. It scribbled across
+	// the rest of wave RAM, which the user saw as a take replacing the kick,
+	// snare and hihat already loaded under the pads.
+	//
+	// Only the WRITE is bounded, not the counter: the firmware polls the
+	// position to decide a take is finished, so pos has to keep advancing
+	// or the poll never completes. And only while recording - ordinary wave
+	// DMA loading a sample from disk uses this same callback and does not
+	// always have a meaningful end programmed.
+	if (m_rec_on && (vptr.end > vptr.start) && ((vptr.start + vptr.pos) >= vptr.end))
+	{
+		// THE REGION IS FULL: STOP, AS THE HARDWARE DOES.
